WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579 align:start position:0%
 
New Orleans mayor that's Mitch Landrieu  is opening up about his decision to  remove four Confederate monuments from  his City  Marilyn drew recently took 60 minutes to  a shed where the statues of generals  robert e lee and PGT Beauregard now Stan  the Democrat also removed statues last  year of Confederate President Jefferson  Davis in the Battle of Liberty Place  monument that statue commemorate an  attempt by white supremacist to  overthrow the city's integrated  government after the Civil War  mayor Landrieu discusses a path he took  toward removing the monuments in his new  book in the shadow of statues a white  southerner confronts history mayor  Landrieu joins us at the table hello  white southerner I was so moved by your  book I have to say I was I was very  moved by it there were so many things  and I didn't know and didn't really  think about until I was reading your  book but this all started from a  conversation with went in Marsalis who  was an old friend of yours it did the  context was of course you know the city  was destroyed by Katrina and thanked the  rest of Americans for helping to stand  back up but as we were building the city  back and reconstructing it the mantra  was don't put it back like it was put it  back the way it should have been if he  would have gotten it right the first  time and so as we begin to prepare for  our 300th anniversary which is in a  couple of weeks by the way I asked  wenting to help me and he said well I'll  help you he said you really ought to  think about taking down the robert e lee  statue and I said why would I do that  and he said well have you ever thought  about who put it there why it's there or  what impact it has on people like me and  then the next thing he said really just  exploded my brain which was that you  know Louis Armstrong left here and  didn't want to come back because of that  statute and when he said that the notion  of the great diaspora five million  african-americans who fled the south  after the Civil War and really just  troubled me greatly and I told him that  I would look into it and it did and you  to put it in context about what it means  though why it is so painful well when  you when you see it from Winton's eyes  so you see it from the eyes of a young  african-american girl in the city that's  60% african-american those monuments  were put up by people for a reason which  was to send a message that  notwithstanding the fact that the  Confederacy lost the war that that idea  was still in the control of the south  and much like Jim Crow logic much like  separate but equal  much like the laws that prohibited  african-americans from following whites  in a car on the highway and passing them  these statutes were put up well after  the Civil War for the specific purpose  of removing the Confederacy which of  course you know was designed to destroy  the nation over the cause of slavery and  the reaction when you did this was you  couldn't find a crane among other well  it was a very emotional reaction we had  a number of long debates in public  hearings in the City Council court races  but after the decision was made legally  to take them down even the a powerful  mayor of a city that was rebuilding the  entire city airport's riverfront schools  I couldn't find anybody to give me a  crane and I couldn't find a crane  operator which really began to introduce  me into the concept of institutional  racism and what that really meant and  you could have the law on your side but  if you didn't have the levers of power  or the money or the equipment you were  basically screwed forgive the language  but you couldn't get anything done and  that story came back to me powerfully  because I've heard that from a lot of my  friends for many years over over a long  time in the book in the book you  mentioned race and you say here's what I  know about race you can't go over it you  can't go under it you can't go around it  you have to go through it that's a  difficult thing to do well it's very  hard and of course dr. King has kind of  showed us the way but there are people  who were still with us so I consider to  be saints like John Lewis who as you may  recall and I talked about this in the  book we're standing on the Edmund Pettus  Bridge and he knew he was about to get  hit by that Sheriff and he never moves  and he's walked us through this  difficult perhaps because we really  don't know how to talk about it in the  country it's a very uncomfortable and  the point that I'm making in the book is  based on my experience it's very hard  but you have to talk about it and you  have to walk through it we have to give  each other a lot of room in the book  really is more of an invitation than it  is a judgment or an accusation for  people to see the world differently  based on what the real history was so  how do you have that conversation with  people who don't immediate who aren't  giving you the crane how do you how do  you have that conversation well you do  it by taking down mind image you do it  by giving speech as you do it by getting  people around the table to actually say  can you see the world from our  perspective and so it's it is the book  is about race but writ large it's about  discrimination it's about really getting  back to what makes us really great  Americans which is judging people based  on that behavior not what they look like  where they come from what their religion  is what their origin of the nation  really on behavior that's the idea of  America which makes us great but you  said Mara whenever you would waver  listen you got death threats you were  threatened yeah when it came to take  those statues down you had to have  police escorts sharpshooters yeah  wearing wearing wearing on what do you  call it the bulletproof vests so they  had to be totally covered it was that  dangerous but you said whenever you  wavered you would think about two  numbers which I thought was interesting  what were their number one of them was  how many African Americans were enslaved  which is around five or six million and  the others how many people were killed  in the Civil War at six hundred thousand  plus more and the sacrifice that they  made and by the way I'm not the first  one that ever tried this they've been so  many people that have had more difficult  struggles than this that are too  numerous to name right now but you could  go back my dad told me well he was  afraid because he was my he had been the  mayor of the city of New Orleans he knew  what the issue of race was about his  life was threatened when he was 29 years  old and I was in utero when he voted  against the segregation package when  Jimmy Davis and a lot of folks were  around so he knew that this was not a  safe thing having said that a lot of  other people have made greatest  sacrifices and I will ever make to this  particular cause so when that happened I  just tried to remember the other people  that that had courage and and just kind  of kept walking and a lot of people  helped this was a this was a communal  laughter this is not something clearly  that I did by myself while the first one  to actually talk about it I loved that  you wanted to be an actor before poet I  absolutely did I still do we dare think  Mitch Landrieu 2020 no ma'am  okay but thanks for thanks for what does  moon think yeah it was pretty sober  about it all - I mean that's a that's  always a tough road right mayor Landrieu  it's always good to see you always good  to have you in the morning thank you so  much for having me  and the shadow of statues is on sale now  wherever you like to buy your books

